  "query_preview": "..Our downtick in monsoonal activity will continue this\nweekend as a strong high pressure system remains centered over \nArizona. This downtick will be driven by drier air working into \nthe region and subsidence under the ridge. Model soundings \nindicate that capping aloft will continue to strengthen, combined\nwith PW dropping to the 20th-40th percentile for early August. \nOverall storm coverage and intensity this weekend will be \nsuppressed. Activity will not shut off completely, still expect \nisolated storms over the higher terrain of eastern Arizona each \nday. \n\nThe primary hazard over the next few days will be heat. The decrease \nin moisture plus above normal heights at 500 mb will lead to the \nwarmest temperatures of the year so far. Low elevation areas south\nof the Mogollon Rim, and the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon\nand Glen/Marble Canyon will see daytime highs approach or exceed \n110 F. The Extreme Heat Warning for these areas has been extended \nuntil Monday, with the Grand Canyon heat warning extended until \nTuesday. The lower elevations of northeastern Arizona will see \nabove normal temperatures up to 100-105 F. The Heat Advisory for \nthese areas has also been extended until Monday. Be sure to drink \nplenty of fluids and avoid prolonged outdoor activity this weekend\nif you can!\n\nThe high is forecast to weaken and elongate by the early to middle \npart of next week. The current forecast calls for a cooling trend \nand uptick in moisture beginning Tuesday-Wednesday. There is still \nplenty of uncertainty at this point in the forecast, and guidance \nvaries on the strength and exact placement of the ridge. However, \nthe overall ensemble trend is for PW to climb back towards another \nperiod of active monsoonal conditions.",
